{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-noisy-grid-inference.md", "content": "# Failed noisy-grid inference: preserve full changed-cell evidence

This attempt stream involved heterogeneous noisy-background demonstrations and a compact third-color motif. Multiple unsupported test candidates were submitted: recoloring the marker-adjacent cells, 4-/8-connected zero components, enclosed zero cells, and finally leaving the grid unchanged. All were rejected. The demos had visibly different changed masks and did not establish a simple uniform flood-fill rule; connectivity and proximity alone were not sufficient evidence.

Reusable lesson: compare every demo's changed and preserved cells before selecting a transformation. Explicitly test competing neighborhood/connectivity rules across complete pairs and inspect exceptions; do not infer global propagation merely from a marker in one example. After binary rejection, discard the whole candidate, not just a guessed boundary. Once the demo cap is exhausted, avoid cycling through local variants or reverting to input unchanged without evidence. This note records failed candidates, not a validated alternative rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-noisy-grid-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stream-session-review.md", "content": "# Session review: noisy heterogeneous demos and rejected guesses

This session illustrates a costly failure pattern: eight demo requests were made consecutively without using each new pair to sharpen the live rule, followed by repeated submissions of speculative color overlays. The demos varied in palette, dimensions, and object structure, so visual resemblance was not enough to justify a shared rule. Each pair needed a changed/preserved-cell comparison and explicit statement of the remaining discriminator before requesting again.

After demos were exhausted, repeated rejections showed that color substitutions and broad overlays were not validated. A wrong answer only rejected the entire candidate; it did not identify cells to change. The useful recovery step is to reset the candidate, build a compact evidence ledger across every demo (trigger, geometric direction, affected cells, preserved marks, palette roles), and test each candidate against the complete set before spending another attempt. Do not add guessed alterations merely to differ from the last answer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/stream-session-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md", "content": "# Rejected global-fill hypothesis: preserve geometry, audit exact changes

In this Continual-ARC instance, several demos used noisy grids with one distinctive-color object. An attempted submission globally flood-filled every zero connected to the marker color, and binary feedback rejected it. The failure illustrates why connectivity alone is not sufficient evidence for recoloring: the demos preserve many background cells, and different examples show selective additions localized to patterned regions rather than blanket access from a seed.

